WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:04.580
The way we travel around
our cities is changing.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.160
We’re having to keep our
distance from one another,

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.000
so that means fewer buses,
trains and cabs.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.280
Cycling has a myriad of benefits for your
health, not to mention to that of a city.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.060
More people on bikes usually means
fewer people in cars.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.730
Fewer cars means better air.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.780
From Berlin to Bogata cycling infrastructure
is popping up or being reinforced.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.340
So is this strange period we’re going through
the smartest time to transform our cities

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.780
into bike-friendly utopias?

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:46.920
I’ve lived in London for well over ten years
and I’ve never ridden a bike here.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.740
Cycling campaigners are
staging a protest over what

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.540
they describe as the failure of London's 
councils to improve safety.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:59.140
Like a lot of people in major cities, I’ve
been put off by the lack of cycling infrastructure

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.860
and the scary headlines.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.400
But with fewer cars on the road I’ve taken
the plunge and bought a bike,

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.540
from Ben here.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:07.400
You alright?

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.560
Yeah, how you doing?

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:16.180
So Londoners are buying bikes like nobody’s
business,

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.520
shops are selling out.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.000
I wonder if we can make it stick?

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:22.480
How are you doing?

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.280
Not too bad, how are you?

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.260
Good

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.020
This is my colleague Feargus O'Sullivan.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.960
I bought this bike about four days ago.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.190
Join the club!

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:33.359
Feargus covers all things mobility in cities.

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:36.590
How important is cycling going to be, for
a city like London, going forward?

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:42.329
It’s simply not going to be safe to fill
public transport at anything like the volumes

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:45.300
that were common before the pandemic.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.240
Something has to change.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.040
Now is a really unusual moment in history.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:55.710
Are we ever going to get a chance to
re-organise a city like this ever again?

00:01:55.710 --> 00:02:01.201
We need to push for a genuine modal shift,
not just because people like cycling,

00:02:01.201 --> 00:02:05.170
but because it's altogether cleaner,
healthier, safer.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:10.330
People's lives are being substantially shortened
in this city, in many cities because of the

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:13.540
presence of really serious airborne pollution.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:17.690
If people cycle, that that level of pollution
is going to fall enormously.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:21.470
The amount of people you could fit safely
into a road space, even with social distancing,

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:25.650
is far, far greater with bikes
than it is with cars,

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:28.640
You can see a cycle lane that appears to be
half empty

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.440
next to a car lane that is
completely full of cars,

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.940
you’ll find that they have
the same number of people.

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:37.420
But one of them is completely gridlocked
and causing pollution

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.580
and the other one is causing no pollution
and it's completely fluid.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.120
You need to think about where people are going
and where they want to go

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.000
and make sure that they're safe
from doorstep to doorstep.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:52.360
Most of us associate a road
with the motor vehicle,

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.260
but actually there was a time
when streets were for people.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.040
Cars have come to totally 
dominate our road space.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:03.860
Even in Amsterdam around 70 percent of the
road space is allotted for cars when they

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:08.250
constitute, I think, less than a quarter of
journeys every day.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:14.510
So even a place that's famous for giving a
huge amount of space and putting cyclists

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:17.980
and pedestrians first cars still own the road.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.900
It's one thing to encourage cycling and be
pro-cycling, but you actually have to make

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:29.820
space for bikes and making that space often
means taking that space away from cars.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:43.860
I’ve managed to find a nice quiet road,
still absolutely full of cars though.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:45.760
They’re everywhere.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:50.880
We’ve got a bit of a strange attachment
to cars - sure, they’re incredibly useful -

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.200
but in cities, they make less sense.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:59.040
The socio-economic cost of cycling is much
lower than that of a car

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:04.970
According to a Danish study it costs society
79 cents for every kilometer driven by a car.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:08.960
Society gains 72 cents per kilometer cycled.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.690
That’s because cycling reduces healthcare
costs and sick leave and driving comes with

00:04:13.690 --> 00:04:16.880
all sorts of extras like parking and fuel.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:20.580
Top cycling cities like 
Amsterdam and Copenhagen,

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:24.140
they’ve managed to prioritise
people over the car.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:28.220
Those places took a decent
amount of time to get there.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:33.160
We’re going to look at somewhere
that took under two years.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.960
That’s Seville in Spain.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:41.960
From under seven thousand trips
a day in 2006,

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:47.240
there were over 72,000 being made
on bikes in Seville by 2011.

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:52.580
So that's a 1000 percentage increase
in just four years.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:55.560
Manuel Calvo, a Spanish socio-ecologist,

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.480
was one of the people behind
Seville’s cycling transformation.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.620
It really is the closest thing you can get
to overnight success

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.380
in making a city cycling-friendly.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:07.140
One of the main factors was the infrastructure.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.940
Around 70 or 80 percent of that
space came from cars,

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.880
actually parking spaces.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.340
We took that road space and we elevated it

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:20.000
and it's placed on the sidewalk platform.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:26.820
We did that because the perceived safety for
the new users would be greater if we did that.

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:30.680
Separating cars and bikes by a
physical barrier like a raised kerb

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.660
is essential campaigners argue,

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:37.640
saying it makes cycling accessible
to people of all ages and all abilities.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:41.420
Building permanent infrastructure also makes
it more likely to last,

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:44.340
especially if anti-cycling
politicians come to power.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:48.320
For them it would be really difficult
to get rid of that curb

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:51.720
on the whole sidewalk platform.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:59.009
So that was political strategy to get that
space for once and forever.

00:05:59.009 --> 00:06:04.050
The result in Seville was a network of cycle
lanes that changed the landscape of the city.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:06.420
The two main lessons that we learned in Seville;

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:09.719
to have a whole network and to have it fast.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:14.309
To create mass infrastructure at lightning
speed normally costs a lot of money.

00:06:14.309 --> 00:06:19.480
Seville's first stage of 80 kilometres of cycle
paths cost under twenty million dollars.

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:24.300
In comparison, Seville’s metro line cost
about 900 million dollars.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.999
It serves 44,000 trips every day, far less
than that of bikes.

00:06:28.999 --> 00:06:31.460
Where you do one kilometer of highway,

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:34.340
you build a hundred kilometers of cycle lanes.

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:36.980
I mean the numbers are just stunning.

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:40.600
The example of Seville shows it is possible to transform
a city

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.840
if you have the political will.

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:47.100
Meanwhile big cities like London are often
criticized for not being brave enough.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:51.340
There’s the bike symbol.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:53.680
Where do we
go now?

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:56.460
Well that’s a dead end.

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:00.400
Oh god, pot holes...

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.620
Doesn’t feel like a
cycle path.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:07.001
As part of its response to the coronavirus,

00:07:07.001 --> 00:07:10.420
the UK government has fast-tracked
two hundred and fifty million pounds

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:12.540
to invest in emergency
infrastructure

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.389
for cyclists and pedestrians.

00:07:14.389 --> 00:07:17.940
Additionally, London’s transport authority
has launched a plan

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:20.820
which it says can potentially accommodate
a ten-fold increase

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:23.690
in cycling when lockdown
restrictions are eased.

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:26.380
These are all temporary emergency
measures at the moment,

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:29.820
we’re creating safe space for many,
many millions more people

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:33.439
to walk and cycle as part of
those journeys instead

00:07:33.439 --> 00:07:36.689
I came from about six, seven miles away.

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:39.150
I found it quite hard to find cycle lanes.

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.160
There's a lot of work that needs to go on.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.589
So the first thing we need is that safe infrastructure.

00:07:43.589 --> 00:07:47.150
But you're right, then we need to make sure
people know how to use it.

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:48.150
Every city is different.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:50.700
You know, while Paris has
got its wide boulevards

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.729
and Berlin has got those big streets,

00:07:52.729 --> 00:07:55.129
we're working here with
narrow medieval lanes so

00:07:55.129 --> 00:07:57.029
we need a plan that works for London,

00:07:57.029 --> 00:08:02.460
We've seen three times more cycling in lockdown
in some parts of London than outside of lockdown,

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.340
so there's that demand there.

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:07.289
Is this something that we can
make permanent in London?

00:08:07.289 --> 00:08:08.900
What happens if we don't take this opportunity?

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:09.979
We've got no choice.

00:08:09.979 --> 00:08:13.999
We’ll end up with gridlock, our emergency
services will get stuck, all our deliveries

00:08:13.999 --> 00:08:17.479
will get jammed up, businesses won’t be
able to get the supplies they need.

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:21.880
But also, I think importantly, 
we’ll have a toxic air crisis.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.020
While the coronavirus is driving up
the number of bike trips,

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:28.300
there’s also a risk it could put
more cars on the road too.

00:08:28.300 --> 00:08:32.780
In China, the use of private cars nearly doubled
when lockdowns ended.

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:37.979
The main reason given for buying a new car
was to avoid the chance of infection.

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:42.900
In London, there are reasons to be hopeful
of a more bike-friendly future.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:47.180
Before the pandemic the city had significantly
increased protected cycle space,

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:50.050
claiming to have tripled the length
of routes in four years.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:53.760
And like other major global cities such as
New York and Hong Kong,

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:56.600
has a population that generally uses public transport

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:57.959
more than anything else.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:01.600
So the main problem isn’t reducing
the number of car journeys,

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:05.260
but actually making our cities
safer and easier to cycle in.

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:08.760
If our urban spaces are to function post-coronavirus,

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.280
they’ll have to implement actions
that are pro-people

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:14.620
and make sure it happens fast.

